{"word": "heartless", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "heartless", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adjective", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈhɑːt.lɪs/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈhɑɹt.lɪs/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "more heartless", "tags": ["comparative"]}, {"word": "most heartless", "tags": ["superlative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Without feeling, emotion, or concern for others; uncaring; cruel.", "tags": [], "examples": ["His heartless actions and cold manner left her saddened and feeling alone."], "quotes": [{"text": "The world is a dangerous, violent place […] and the only true refuge from this heartless world is the family.", "reference": "1982 August 14, Michael Bronski, “If You Hated The Book You'll Loathe The Movie”, in Gay Community News, volume 10, number 5, page 11:"}, {"text": "It's safer to be heartless than mindless. The history of the world is the history of the triumph of the heartless over the mindless.", "reference": "1986 January 23, Antony Jay, Jonathan Lynn, “The Smoke Screen”, in Yes, Prime Minister, season 1, episode 3, spoken by Humphrey Appleby (Nigel Hawthorne):"}, {"text": "Mr. Burns is similarly perfectly cast as a heartless capitalist willing to do anything for a quick buck, even if it means endangering the lives of those around him and Marge elegantly rounds out the main cast as a good, pure-hearted and overly indulgent woman who sees the big, good heart (literally and metaphorically) of a monstrous man-brute.", "reference": "2012 April 29, Nathan Rabin, “TV: Review: THE SIMPSONS (CLASSIC): “Treehouse of Horror III” (season 4, episode 5; originally aired 10/29/1992)”, in The A.V. Club, archived from the original on 15 Aug 2012:"}], "synonyms": ["cold-hearted", "uncordial", "brutal", "callous", "cold", "cold-eyed", "cruel", "dour", "flint-hearted", "forbidding", "frosty", "grim", "hard", "har